## Detailed Connections

### RFID Reader (RC522)
- **SPI bus:** D10 (SS), D11 (MOSI), D12 (MISO), D13 (SCK) — these are hardware SPI pins on the UNO
- **Control:** D9 (RST)
- **Power:** 3.3V ONLY (not 5V) — the RC522 is 3.3V tolerant only
- **Ground:** GND

### Keypad (4x4 matrix)
- Rows connect to D2–D5 (pull one row LOW to read)
- Columns connect to D6–D8 and A0 (read which columns are LOW)
- This uses the standard Keypad library matrix scanning
- No power connections needed for the keypad itself (it's just switches)

### PIR Sensor (HC-SR501)
- **VCC:** 5V (the HC-SR501 requires 5V)
- **OUT:** A1 (HIGH when motion detected)
- **GND:** GND
- Note: The sensor has a warm-up period (~1 minute on first power). It may report HIGH on startup — this is normal

### Buzzer
- This is an **active buzzer** (it contains an oscillator), not a passive one
- **Positive (+):** A2 (set HIGH to activate)
- **Negative (−):** GND
- Do NOT use PWM — just digital on/off

### Relay (5V Songle SRD-05VDC)
- This relay is driven by a **transistor driver circuit** on the breadboard (2N2222 NPN transistor + 1N4x diode + 1kΩ resistor)
- **Arduino A3 → 1kΩ resistor → Transistor Base**
- **Transistor Emitter → GND**
- **Transistor Collector → Relay Coil Pin A** (via the breadboard)
- **Relay Coil Pin B → 5V rail** (via the breadboard)
- **Diode:** Cathode (banded end) → 5V side; Anode → Collector side (freewheeling diode to protect against back-EMF)
- **Relay output (COM/NO/NC):** The relay output controls the simulated lock — see the hardware photo for the final wiring

### RTC (DS1307)
- **SDA:** A4 (I2C data) — shared with any other I2C devices
- **SCL:** A5 (I2C clock) — shared with any other I2C devices
- **VCC:** 5V
- **GND:** GND
- The module includes a battery holder so the clock continues even after power-off

## Shared Power Rails

Since Arduino pins are limited, SecureGate uses two shared power rails on the breadboard:
- **5V rail:** Powers the PIR sensor, relay coil, and RTC
- **GND rail:** Distributes ground to all components

This reduces wiring complexity without changing functionality.

## Testing Connections

After wiring, before uploading code:
1. **Visually verify** every wire matches the pin assignments above
2. **Check for loose wires** or touching adjacent pins (especially around the relay driver circuit)
3. **Power on** and open Serial Monitor — you should see boot messages confirming the setup

If something doesn't work, first re-check the wiring; ~99% of issues on breadboard builds are wiring-related.
